                    "a": "Model poem on 'Do not judge people by their appearance and profession' (three stanzas):\n\nA tailor's son may yet be wise,\nThough poor and plain in dress he be;\nFor wisdom does not wear a disguise,\nNor lives in halls of high degree.\n\nA king may sit upon his throne,\nAnd yet be foolish, proud and blind;\nWhile shepherds, living all alone,\nMay hold a sharper, wiser mind.\n\nSo judge no man by coat or crown,\nBy trade or title, gold or birth;\nFor wisdom often wears a gown\nOf simple cloth, of modest worth. (Model answer — compose your own poem in three stanzas.)",
