                  {
                    "a": "(b) Devonian — The earliest vascular plants were relatively small and probably confined to swampy areas, and after that a rapid diversification of plants occurred gradually; the Devonian is the period of the great diversification of vascular plants on land. (The book does not name the period directly in the text.)",
                    "q": "(i) The rapid diversification of plants occurred in the period:\n(a) Silurian\n(b) Devonian\n(c) Carboniferous\n(d) Paleozoic"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(d) Sporopollenin — Sporopollenin, composed chiefly of carotenoids, makes the spores of land plants tough and flexible and resistant to biodegradation, and protects them from attack by grazing organisms and microorganisms.",
                    "q": "(ii) Spores of land plants tough, flexible and resistant to biodegradation by:\n(a) Lignin\n(b) Chitin\n(c) Waxes\n(d) Sporopollenin"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(d) Bryophytes — Bryophytes include the liverworts (Class Hepaticae), the hornworts (Class Anthocerotae) and the mosses (Class Musci).",
                    "q": "(iii) Hepaticae, Anthocerotae and Moss are the classes of:\n(a) Angiosperms\n(b) Gymnosperms\n(c) Tracheophytes\n(d) Bryophytes"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(a) Heterogamy — Heterogamy is the most successful kind of reproduction that has evolved in bryophytes: the production of two different types of gametes, one male (motile) and the other female (non-motile) full of stored food.",
                    "q": "(iv) The most successful kind of reproduction is:\n(a) Heterogamy\n(b) Oogamy\n(c) Anisogamy\n(d) Isogamy"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(a) Sporophyte — The dominant generation of living vascular plants is the sporophyte, existing as a free-living plant, while the gametophyte is smaller, short-lived and degenerates once the sporophyte is established.",
                    "q": "(v) The dominant generation of living vascular plants existing as free-living plants is:\n(a) Sporophyte\n(b) Gametophyte\n(c) Tracheophyte\n(d) Pteridophyte"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(c) Psilophyta — The Psilopsida (whisk ferns, e.g. Psilotum) are the most primitive group of living vascular plants; they are considered living fossils because of their apparent similarities to the fossil plant Rhynia.",
                    "q": "(vi) The most primitive living vascular plants is:\n(a) Lycophyta\n(b) Sphenophyta\n(c) Psilophyta\n(d) Filicinophyta"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(d) Prothallus — The gametophyte of a fern is reduced to a small, simple prothallus: an independent autotrophic, heart-shaped, dorsoventrally flattened structure lying prostrate on a wet substratum.",
                    "q": "(vii) The gametophyte of Fern is called:\n(a) Archegonium\n(b) Antheridium\n(c) Cones\n(d) Prothallus"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(c) Angiosperms — Double fertilization is a special type of fertilization which occurs only in angiospermic plants: one sperm fuses with the ovum to form the oospore (zygote) and the other sperm fuses with the secondary nucleus to form the triploid endosperm nucleus.",
                    "q": "(viii) Double fertilization is the characteristic feature of:\n(a) Bryophytes\n(b) Gymnosperms\n(c) Angiosperms\n(d) Ferns"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(c) Pines (printed 'Pins') — Pines are a source of resins, turpentine and pine oil.",
                    "q": "(ix) Resins, turpentine and pine oil are obtained from:\n(a) Rose\n(b) Onion\n(c) Pins\n(d) Banana"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(a) Spathes — In a spadix inflorescence the flowers are covered over by one or many large bracts called spathes (e.g. banana). (The book prints 'brackets' for 'bracts'.)",
                    "q": "(x) In banana tree, flowers are covered over by one or many large brackets called:\n(a) Spathes\n(b) Spadix\n(c) Capitulum\n(d) Palea"
                  }
