                    "a": "Diploblastic animals have only two germ layers, ectoderm and endoderm, with a jelly-like mesoglea between them; they have the tissue level of organization, e.g. cnidarians. Triploblastic animals have three germ layers — ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm — from which all other structures are derived; they have the organ and system level of organization, e.g. Platyhelminthes to Chordata.",
                    "q": "7. Why diploblastic animals are different from the triploblastic?"
