                    "a": "Let the three mesh currents be I1, I2 and I3, all clockwise as shown in the figure. The current through the 10 Ω resistor is I2.\nMesh 1 (left loop): 5I1 + 15(I1 − I2) = 4\n20I1 − 15I2 = 4 ... (i)\nMesh 2 (middle loop): 15(I2 − I1) + 10I2 + 8(I2 − I3) = 0\n−15I1 + 33I2 − 8I3 = 0 ... (ii)\nMesh 3 (right loop): 8(I3 − I2) + 12I3 + 6 = 0\n−8I2 + 20I3 = −6 ... (iii)\nFrom (i): I1 = (4 + 15I2)/20\nFrom (iii): I3 = (8I2 − 6)/20\nSubstituting in (ii):\n−15 × (4 + 15I2)/20 + 33I2 − 8 × (8I2 − 6)/20 = 0\n−3 − 11.25I2 + 33I2 − 3.2I2 + 2.4 = 0\n−0.6 + 18.55I2 = 0\nI2 = 0.6/18.55\nI2 = 0.0323 A\nSo the current through the 10 Ω resistor is 0.0323 A, flowing clockwise (from left to right through the 10 Ω resistor).\n(Note: the book prints 0.0341 A, which does not satisfy the mesh equations; the correct value is 0.0323 A.)",
                    "q": "7. By using KVL find Current flowing through 10 Ω resistance. (Ans:I10Ω =0.0341A(clockwise))"
