          {
            "exercises": [
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "Oscillation is a to-and-fro motion about a fixed mean position, e.g. a swinging pendulum or a vibrating spring.",
                    "q": "What is oscillation? Give an example."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Periodic motion is motion that repeats itself after a fixed interval of time. Every oscillatory motion is periodic, but not every periodic motion is oscillatory.",
                    "q": "What is periodic motion?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Simple harmonic motion is an oscillatory motion in which the acceleration is directly proportional to the displacement from the mean position and is directed towards the mean position: a = −ω^2x.",
                    "q": "Define simple harmonic motion."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The time period is the time taken for one complete oscillation. Its SI unit is the second (s).",
                    "q": "What is the time period of an oscillation?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Frequency is the number of oscillations per second. Its SI unit is the hertz (Hz). Frequency and period are related by f = 1/T.",
                    "q": "What is frequency? Write its SI unit."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Amplitude is the maximum displacement of the oscillating body from its mean position.",
                    "q": "What is amplitude?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Additional Questions — Oscillation and SHM"
              },
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "The angular frequency is ω = 2πf = 2π/T. Its SI unit is rad/s.",
                    "q": "What is angular frequency?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The displacement of a particle in SHM is x = Asin(ωt + φ), where A is the amplitude, ω the angular frequency and φ the initial phase.",
                    "q": "Write the equation for the displacement of a particle in SHM."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The velocity in SHM is v = ω√(A^2 − x^2). It is maximum (v_max = ωA) at the mean position and zero at the extreme positions.",
                    "q": "Write the formula for the velocity of a particle in SHM."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The acceleration in SHM is a = −ω^2x. It is zero at the mean position and maximum (a_max = ω^2A) at the extreme positions.",
                    "q": "Write the formula for the acceleration of a particle in SHM."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Phase is the state of an oscillating particle at any instant, measured as an angle (ωt + φ). It determines the position and the direction of motion.",
                    "q": "What is phase in SHM?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Additional Questions — SHM: Displacement, Velocity and Acceleration"
              },
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "For a mass-spring system, T = 2π√(m/k). The period increases with the mass and decreases with the spring constant.",
                    "q": "Write the period of a mass-spring system."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "For a simple pendulum, T = 2π√(l/g). The period increases with the length and decreases with g.",
                    "q": "Write the period of a simple pendulum."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "A seconds pendulum is a pendulum whose period is 2 seconds (one second for each swing). Its length is about 1 m.",
                    "q": "What is a seconds pendulum?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The period of a simple pendulum does not depend on the mass of the bob or (for small amplitudes) on the amplitude.",
                    "q": "On what does the period of a simple pendulum NOT depend?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The period of a pendulum is shorter on Earth than on the Moon because g is larger on Earth (T ∝ 1/√g).",
                    "q": "Is the period of a pendulum longer on the Earth or on the Moon?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "If the length of a pendulum is quadrupled, its period is doubled (T ∝ √l).",
                    "q": "What happens to the period if the length of a pendulum is quadrupled?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Additional Questions — Simple Pendulum and Mass-Spring System"
              },
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "The total energy in SHM is E = (1/2)mω^2A^2. It is constant if there is no damping.",
                    "q": "Write the formula for the total energy of a particle in SHM."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The kinetic energy is maximum at the mean position and zero at the extreme positions.",
                    "q": "Where is the kinetic energy maximum in SHM?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The potential energy is maximum at the extreme positions and zero at the mean position.",
                    "q": "Where is the potential energy maximum in SHM?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The total energy is proportional to the square of the amplitude: if the amplitude is doubled, the energy becomes four times as large.",
                    "q": "How does the total energy change if the amplitude is doubled?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "In SHM, energy is continuously exchanged between kinetic and potential forms, but their sum (the total mechanical energy) stays constant.",
                    "q": "How is energy conserved in SHM?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Additional Questions — Energy in SHM"
              },
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "Damping is the gradual decrease in the amplitude of an oscillation due to the loss of energy by friction or air resistance.",
                    "q": "What is damping?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Free oscillations are oscillations at the natural frequency of the system with no external driving force and no energy supplied.",
                    "q": "What are free oscillations?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Forced oscillations are oscillations produced by an external periodic driving force; the system oscillates at the frequency of the driver.",
                    "q": "What are forced oscillations?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Resonance occurs when the driving frequency equals the natural frequency of the system, so the amplitude becomes maximum.",
                    "q": "What is resonance? When does it occur?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "A shock absorber in a vehicle is an application of damped oscillations: it dissipates the energy of the jerks as heat and reduces the bouncing of the vehicle.",
                    "q": "What is a shock absorber? Give one application of damped oscillations."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The quality factor Q measures the sharpness of resonance: a high Q means a sharp resonance (narrow, tall peak), a low Q means a broad, flat resonance.",
                    "q": "What is the quality factor?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Additional Questions — Damping, Forced Oscillations and Resonance"
              }
            ],
            "name": "Additional Questions",
            "num": "11.3"
          }
